{
 "id": "2025-tural-restorative",
 "name": "Restorative Consultation Rooms",
 "description": "Which interior design choices make a room for mental-health care feel restorative? Tural & Tural (2025, IJERPH) rendered one 15 × 15 ft consultation room (couch, two chairs, coffee table) and changed one thing at a time: window size (0%, 33%, 66%, 100% of the wall), the view out of the window (desert, water, woods, lawn, from HDR 360° photographs), the materials (adaptive-reuse brick with hardwood and steel decking; concrete; a natural / biophilic set with a green wall, timber ceiling and nature-patterned carpet; textured walls with resilient flooring), and the ceiling shape (curved, flat, angled, vaulted). 35 design students rated each room once on a screen and once in VR with a single item, \"Please rate the degree of restorativeness this setting offers you\" (7-point scale). On screen, water views scored highest of the views (5.11) and lawn lowest (4.06); natural materials beat concrete and resilient finishes (4.46 vs 2.77 and 2.94); the vaulted ceiling scored highest and the flat ceiling lowest (4.29 vs 3.23). VR ratings were higher than screen ratings for most rooms, and the two correlated strongly. This template runs the screen condition with the paper's own renderings. Analysis as in the paper: a repeated-measures ANOVA per set with post-hoc comparisons; if you also collect VR or in-person ratings, compare them with paired t-tests and correlations as in the paper's Table 1. Differences from the original study: (1) the window-size set is not included, because the paper prints only the 100% rendering (Fig 1a) and diagrams for 33% and 66%; the template has the other three sets (12 rooms); (2) the images are the figure versions (about 1300 × 730 pixels for views and materials, 900 × 490 for ceilings), not the 2160 × 1440 originals; the ceiling renderings are smaller and less detailed; (3) the paper presented sets in sequence and does not give the order within a set; the template uses the order of the paper's Table 1; (4) the scale anchors (1 = not restorative at all, 7 = extremely restorative) are our wording; (5) the VR condition is not part of the template; (6) the background questions are optional and our own.",
